WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.120
The hybrid test pinned the fault on the lake copy's own DNA.

2
00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:11.400
Pitx1 RNA is missing where the pelvis forms.

3
00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:15.760
So which stretch of DNA is the broken pelvic switch?

4
00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:18.380
And how would you prove it?

5
00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:25.320
The colours are the same: blue for marine, orange for Paxton, gold for Pitx1 RNA.

6
00:00:26.450 --> 00:00:31.450
Here is the suspect, a stretch of noncoding DNA just upstream of the gene.

7
00:00:32.710 --> 00:00:33.990
It was first called Pel.

8
00:00:34.970 --> 00:00:38.450
A second switch was found later, so this one became PelA.

9
00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:40.970
Three tests put it on trial.

10
00:00:42.083 --> 00:00:43.843
First test: a reporter.

11
00:00:43.843 --> 00:00:46.583
Cut out the suspect DNA.

12
00:00:47.523 --> 00:00:51.563
Join it to a short start signal and a gene for a visible marker.

13
00:00:52.403 --> 00:00:56.603
Put that into fish embryos, and watch where the marker turns on.

14
00:00:58.933 --> 00:01:03.453
The marine version of PelA drove the marker in the developing pelvis.

15
00:01:04.553 --> 00:01:06.693
The Paxton version carries a deletion.

16
00:01:08.013 --> 00:01:12.353
It gave pelvic expression in none of the fish tested, as the counter shows.

17
00:01:13.517 --> 00:01:16.357
So the Paxton switch drives no pelvic glow.

18
00:01:17.617 --> 00:01:20.257
Does that prove the fish's own pelvis needs PelA?

19
00:01:26.833 --> 00:01:32.733
Not yet. A reporter shows what a sequence can drive, inside a construct.

20
00:01:33.953 --> 00:01:36.453
It does not show that the body depends on it.

21
00:01:37.493 --> 00:01:39.233
For that, you change the fish itself.

22
00:01:40.367 --> 00:01:42.367
Each test answers its own question.

23
00:01:43.787 --> 00:01:45.907
A reporter asks what a sequence can drive.

24
00:01:47.267 --> 00:01:50.107
Adding the switch back asks whether that can restore form.

25
00:01:51.487 --> 00:01:54.807
Removing it from the animal asks whether form depends on it.

26
00:01:55.750 --> 00:01:57.290
Rescue comes closer.

27
00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:04.690
Marine PelA, driving Pitx1, was put into fertilized eggs from another pelvic-reduced

28
00:02:04.690 --> 00:02:08.650
lake. Those fish normally grow no pelvic spine.

29
00:02:10.270 --> 00:02:14.670
Compared with uninjected siblings, pelvic spine growth was variably enhanced.

30
00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:17.150
Now the wild fish.

31
00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:22.210
Many pelvic-reduced populations carry deletions at PelA.

32
00:02:23.470 --> 00:02:25.830
The deletions differ in where they start and stop.

33
00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:29.230
So the same switch was lost more than once.

34
00:02:30.250 --> 00:02:35.830
In one survey, deletions turned up in most pelvic-reduced populations, as the tally

35
00:02:35.830 --> 00:02:40.330
shows. None of the pelvis-complete populations had them.

36
00:02:41.410 --> 00:02:43.130
Common, then, but not universal.

37
00:02:44.610 --> 00:02:47.310
Most of the others showed signs of another route.

38
00:02:48.350 --> 00:02:50.450
Different deletions, same switch.

39
00:02:51.890 --> 00:02:57.150
Pelvic reduction evolved again and again by losing one enhancer, in many lakes but

40
00:02:57.150 --> 00:03:02.070
not all. The deletions hit the switch, not the Pitx1 protein.
